Repository root is the current directory; read files and run read-only python on raw records
(outputs/sota_sprint/twin_null/records.jsonl, data/sprint_twin_losses/*.npz). Begin with
"Reviewer-Model: <exact-model-id>".

Intended claim (C2 revised, after your earlier "partial" verdict asked for known-null real streams):
"In the tested stationary Markov marginal-null family (synthetic), most powered certified ports violate
the registered false-promotion bound somewhere between rho=.8 and .95, whereas A5b remains below it.
On real persistent streams with a known (exchangeable-twin) null, every one of 9 powered certified
published ports also exceeds the bound on at least one dataset (up to .43), and an attachable restart
monitor (RMM) reaches .14-.62; A5b stays at .000-.058 point estimate but its upper bound exceeds the
bound on the smallest dataset. These excess promotions mostly follow the twin that realised lower loss
within the episode, so they violate the marginal guarantee with small decision harm. A5b's robustness
costs substantial power on real streams with a genuinely better challenger. Together these show an
empirical validity-power trade-off for certified deployment under real serial dependence, not a theorem."

Experiments: registered protocol docs/sota_sprint/p2_known_null_protocol.md (commit 36d9796, before any
build); builder scripts/sota_sprint/twin_null.py; analysis scripts/sota_sprint/analyze_twin_null.py;
results docs/sota_sprint/validation/twin_null_results.{md,json}; earlier synthetic and real-power results
docs/sota_sprint/validation/{validation_results.md,real_results.md,real_persistence.json}.

Known caveats: comparators are ports; twins are exchangeable only over the split randomisation (per-
split realised advantages are small but nonzero); episodes are consecutive windows (clustered by split);
harm per false promotion ~ one switch; K2 failed narrowly on the smallest dataset.

